== Collectible value ==

In 1997, [[United States Congress|Congress]] passed the 50 States Commemorative Coin Program Act, which instructed the creation of the 50 State quarters series to "honor the unique Federal Republic of 50 States that comprise the United States; and to promote the diffusion of knowledge among the youth of the United States about the individual states, their history and geography, and the rich diversity of the national heritage...", and to encourage "young people and their families to collect memorable tokens of all of the States for the face value of the coins."<ref>{{cite web |title=Public Law 105-124: 50 States Commemorative Coin Program Act |publisher=United States Congress via United States Mint |date=1997-12-01 |url=http://www.usmint.gov/mint_programs/50sq_program/index.cfm?action=us1claw }} {{Dead link|date=October 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref>

[[File:InGodWeRust.jpg|thumb|right|Coin with partially rubbed off "In God We Trust" motto]]

While mintage totals of the various designs vary widely—Virginia quarters are almost 20 times as abundant as the Northern Marianas quarters—none of the regular circulating issues are rare enough to become a valuable investment.

There was, however, a measure of collector interest over [[Die (manufacturing)|die]] errors in the Wisconsin quarter. Some designs from the Denver mint feature corn without a smaller leaf, others feature a small leaf pointing upwards, and still others have the leaf bending down.<ref name="HR3885">{{cite web |url=http://www.snopes.com/business/money/quarter.asp |website=Snopes.com|title =Wisconsin Quarter Error|first =David|last = Mikkelson|date = 16 May 2011 }}</ref> A set of all three quarters sold on [[eBay]] in February 2005 for $300 and initially saw significant increases, such as $1500 for individual coins, but as of February 2020 [[Professional Coin Grading Service|PCGS]] lists the value of MS-62 specimens from $92 to $130 each.<ref>{{cite web |title=Washington 50 States Quarters Price Guide |url=https://www.pcgs.com/prices/detail/washington-50-states-quarters/720/most-active |website=pcgs.com |publisher=Collectors Universe, Inc. |access-date= 14 February 2020}}</ref>

Another die cast error ran with the first Delaware quarters. Being the first model of state quarter made, the mint gave it a disproportionate weight causing vending machines to not accept it. The quarter die was quickly fixed. Some Delaware quarters appeared without the last ''E'', now saying, "THE FIRST STAT". There also exists a die crack on the Delaware known as the "spitting horse" variety, which is caused by a die crack near the horses' mouth, resembling a horse spitting.<ref>{{cite web | last=Bennett | first=Laura | title=1999 Delaware Spitting Horse Quarter – Error Coin Value & Identification | publisher=Coin ID Scanner | date=2025-11-30 | url=https://coin-identifier.com/blog/coins-overview/1999-delaware-state-quarter | access-date=2026-08-30}}</ref>

A major error occurred in 2000 when the reverse die of a [[Sacagawea dollar]] was combined with the obverse die of a state quarter on dollar-coin planchets to form what is known as a "[[Mule (coin)|mule]]".  As of August 2019, only 19 of [[2000 Sacagawea dollar – Washington quarter mule|these specimens]], produced on dollar planchets, are known to have escaped from the Mint.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.coinfacts.com/error_coins/mules/washington_sacajawea_mule.htm |title=Washington Quarter/ Sacajawea Dollar Mule |publisher=Coinfacts.com |access-date=2013-08-02}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Gilkes |first1=Paul |title=19th known double denomination mule error coin coming to auction |url=https://www.coinworld.com/news/us-coins/19th-known-double-denomination-mule-error-coin-coming-to-auction |website=www.coinworld.com |publisher=Coin World |access-date=11 May 2020}}</ref><ref name=Weinberg>{{cite web |last=Fred Weinberg & Co. |title=2000-P "Mule" Sacagawea Dollar Reverse w/ States Quarter Obverse |url=https://www.fredweinberg.com/error-news/ |publisher=Fred Weinberg & Co. |access-date=18 February 2018 |date=July 2017 |archive-date=May 14, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190514204907/https://www.fredweinberg.com/error-news/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

A 2005 Minnesota double die quarter, as well as a 2005 Minnesota quarter with extra trees (another die error), have both triggered numismatic interest. An unusual die break on some 2005 Kansas quarters created a humpback bison.<ref name="coinworld">{{cite journal|title=Coin World |journal=Coin World |url=http://www.coinworldonline.com/ |access-date=2007-02-02 |date=October 17, 2005 |quote=no |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070127233034/http://coinworldonline.com/ |archive-date=27 January 2007 |url-status=live }}</ref> Relatively more common are Kansas quarters bearing the motto "IN GOD WE RUST."<ref>{{cite web|author=Susan Headley |title=In God We Rust&nbsp;– A State Quarter Error Caused by a Filled Die Strike Through |work=About.com |access-date=2007-08-11 |url=http://coins.about.com/od/uscoins/a/error_quarter_r.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070908090408/http://coins.about.com/od/uscoins/a/error_quarter_r.htm |archive-date=8 September 2007 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The United States produces [[proof coinage]] in circulating base metal and, since 1992, in separately sold sets with the dimes, quarters, and half-dollars in silver.  For the silver issues, the 1999 set is the most valuable, being the first year of the series and with a relatively small mintage, although prices have significantly decreased since the 50 State Quarters Program ended. The set in base metal, of this or any other year, is worth only a fraction as much.  The silver proof sets of later years, while having some intrinsic and collector worth, are also priced far lower.  The public is cautioned to research prices before buying advertised state quarter year or proof sets.

In general, the program increased interest in quarter and general coin collecting.<ref>{{cite web|title=State Quarters|url=http://www.researchbooth.com/categories/hobbies/treasure_hunting/state_quarters.php|website=ResearchBooth.com|access-date=October 21, 2011|archive-date=April 25, 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120425082850/http://www.researchbooth.com/categories/hobbies/treasure_hunting/state_quarters.php|url-status=dead}}</ref> Large numbers of advertisements, quarter products and quarter information were available during the years the program ran. [[Home Shopping Network]], [[Franklin Mint]], and [[Littleton Coin Company]] were among the most prominent in advertising space.

== Seigniorage ==
{{Further information|Seigniorage}}

Since the 50 State Quarters Program was expected to increase public demand for quarters which would be collected and taken out of circulation, the Mint used economic models to estimate the additional seigniorage the program would produce. These estimates established a range of $2.6 billion to $5.1 billion. (At the end of the program, the Mint estimated the actual increase in seigniorage to be $3 billion.) The Mint also estimated the program would earn $110 million in additional numismatic profits. (The final, post-program estimate was $136.2 million.) The Mint used these estimates to support the proposed program, and the legislation enacting the 50 States Quarters program cited these estimates.<ref name="StateQuarters" />

== Satire ==

*On July 25, 2001, ''[[The Onion]]'' ran a satirical news story titled "Collecting All 50 State Quarters Senior's Only Reason To Remain Alive".<ref name="onion">{{cite news |url=https://theonion.com/collecting-all-50-state-quarters-seniors-only-reason-to-1819566097/ |title=Collecting All 50 State Quarters Senior's Only Reason To Remain Alive |access-date=2016-09-02 |work=[[The Onion]] | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20150615044417/http://www.theonion.com/article/collecting-all-50-state-quarters-seniors-only-reas-301| archive-date= 15 June 2015 | url-status= live}}</ref> On May 4, 2005, it ran a further story titled "U.S. Mint Gears Up To Issue Commemorative County Pennies".<ref name="onion2">{{cite news |url=http://www.theonion.com/content/node/31003 |title=U.S. Mint Gears Up To Issue Commemorative County Pennies |access-date=2007-02-02 |work=[[The Onion]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070204040741/http://www.theonion.com/content/node/31003 |archive-date=2007-02-04 |url-status=dead }}</ref>
*The ''[[Late Night with Conan O'Brien]]'' [[television show]] aired several segments about fictional satirical designs for new state quarters.<ref>''Late Night with Conan O'Brien''. NBC. Aired 2005-10-21.</ref><ref>''Late Night with Conan O'Brien''. NBC. Aired 2006-01-18.</ref><ref>''Late Night with Conan O'Brien''. NBC. Aired 2006-04-28.</ref><ref>''Late Night with Conan O'Brien''. NBC. Aired 2006-06-21.</ref><ref>''Late Night with Conan O'Brien''. NBC. Aired 2006-09-25.</ref><ref>''Late Night with Conan O'Brien''. NBC. Aired 2007-02-05.</ref>
*Sculptor Daniel Carr, whose designs were used for the New York and Rhode Island state quarters and whose concept was adapted for the Maine state quarter, has created a series of parody quarters making light of the state quarter concept.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.dc-coin.com/index.asp?PageAction=VIEWCATS&Category=3 |title=Parody State Quarters, Etc |publisher=Dc-coin.com |access-date=2013-08-02}}</ref>

